// =======================================================
// tests/test_x509.cst — tls/x509.cst
//
// Real certificates, pulled from real sites. There is no vector suite for
// X.509, so the parser was written against a corpus of 62 certificates from 20
// sites and validated by comparing every field it extracts — notBefore,
// notAfter, the CA flag, key algorithm, signature algorithm, SAN count and
// first SAN — against openssl on all 62. What is embedded here is the subset
// that covers the shapes: an EC leaf, its RSA-signed issuer, and a leaf
// carrying 163 names in its SubjectAltName.
//
// The corpus is what set the design. It said keyUsage and basicConstraints are
// marked critical on every single certificate, so a parser obeying §4.2's
// "reject unrecognised critical extensions" has to understand those two or it
// rejects the whole internet. It said SANs run to 163 entries, so they are
// iterated rather than collected. And it said every validity field in the wild
// is UTCTime — GeneralizedTime is handled on the strength of RFC 5280 alone,
// which is worth knowing rather than assuming.
//
// Exit 0 = all pass. Non-zero = the numbered check that failed.
// =======================================================

use "../tls/x509.cst" as x509;

